## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical OTCA telemetry design skills, combining privacy compliance, multi-tenancy, and real-time pipeline constraints in a live streaming context.

## Key Concepts Tested
- OTCA telemetry patterns for streaming services
- Per-tenant residency and data isolation
- PII redaction at ingestion point
- Regional Kafka architecture with S3 Parquet sinks
- Tenant/date partitioning strategies
- Quota enforcement and traffic sampling
- Retention policies and lifecycle management
- End-to-end latency measurement

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

function redactPII(userId) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(userId).digest('hex');
}

function partitionKey(event) {
  return `${event.tenant_id}/${event.region}/${event.date}`;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design privacy-first OTCA paths that enforce data residency, tenant isolation, and masking, going beyond basic streaming.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA data contracts, regional immutable storage, global masked aggregates
- Per-tenant masking (tokenization, redaction) and tenant-key management
- Data lineage, access controls, and residency verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskRecord(record, tenantKey) {
  const masked = { ...record };
  if (masked.email) masked.email = hash(masked.email + tenantKey);
  if (masked.phone) masked.phone = redact(masked.phone);
  // redact other PII fields as needed
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test masking correctness and residency under synthetic bursts?
- How do you handle schema evolution for masked fields across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes OTCA (Operational Technology Control Authority) governance for experimentation data, ensuring privacy, regulatory residency, and operational safety in real-time feature flag telemetry.

## Key Concepts
- OTCA residency enforcement with per-tenant data isolation
- Event schema design for flag evaluations
- Regional Kafka topics as ingestion layer with a central immutable data lake
- Consent enforcement at the tenant configuration level
- Privacy budgets, sampling strategies, and bias detection mechanisms
- Rollback testing procedures and failure handling patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example event shape
const event = {
  tenant_id: 't123',
  region: 'eu-west',
  feature_id: 'new-ui',
  variant: 'A',
  timestamp: 1699900000000,
  device_id_hash: 'a1b2c3d4e5f6'
};

// Regional Kafka producer
async function emitFlagEvent(event) {
  const topic = `flag-events-${event.region}`;
  await kafka.producer().send({
    topic,
    messages: [{ value: JSON.stringify(event) }]
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a data catalog system with OTCA residency, lineage tracking, privacy masking, and cross-region compliance. It tests knowledge of OpenLineage integration, data modeling, privacy controls, and multi-region architecture—skills distinct from typical telemetry pipeline work.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region OTCA isolation with per-tenant residency
- OpenLineage-based lineage propagation
- On-read privacy masking for sensitive metadata
- Regional versus central store architecture with audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for regional catalog with privacy masking
class RegionalCatalog {
  constructor(region, tenantId) {
    this.region = region;
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.privacyMasker = new PrivacyMasker();
  }

  async getMetadata(datasetId) {
    const metadata = await this.fetchMetadata(datasetId);
    return this.privacyMasker.mask(metadata, this.tenantId);
  }
}

// OpenLineage event emission for lineage tracking
function emitLineageEvent(jobName, inputs, outputs) {
  const event = new RunEvent({
    eventType: 'COMPLETE',
    job: new Job({ namespace: 'regional-catalog', name: jobName }),
    inputs: inputs.map(i => new Dataset({ namespace: 'otca', name: i })),
    outputs: outputs.map(o => new Dataset({ namespace: 'otca', name: o }))
  });
  openLineageClient.emit(event);
}
```
